                <pb facs="0509" n="397" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg397"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/7342: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Lockhart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-01T12:00:00+08:00">November 1 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >3</hi>], 1935—12 noon</date>.<lb/>[Received November 3—7
                        a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>114. Embassy’s 105, October 30, noon, and 113, November 2, noon. It is stated on
                    seemingly good authority that General Shang Chen, General Sung Cheh-yuan and the
                    Peiping branch of the Political Council have replied to the Japanese <hi
                        rendition="#italic">démarche</hi>, under instructions from Ho Ying-chin,
                    with an assurance that the matter covered by the complaint would be
                    satisfactorily adjusted; that the Council has caused the arrest of some
                    suspects, as stated in Embassy’s 113, November 2, noon, and has issued strict
                    instructions to suppress all anti-Japanese activities. Press reports betray that
                    a considerable number of “Blue Shirts” actually left Peiping for the south
                    within the last few days.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Nanking, by mail to Tokyo and Tientsin.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lockhart</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
